## What the firmware does

One ESP32 board plays two roles over a single MQTT connection:

- **The controller itself** — reports momentary button-press events on its own
  `state` topic (a Last Will marks the whole board offline if it disconnects).
- **Every light circuit wired to it** — drives each light's output pin,
  accepts `{"on":true|false}` commands from the server, and reports state.

Physical buttons keep working with no Wi-Fi/server — manual operation always
has priority (project principle, see `docs/README.md`).

## 1. Prerequisites

- Arduino IDE 2.x (or `arduino-cli`) with the **esp32 by Espressif Systems**
  board package installed (Boards Manager).
- The **PubSubClient** library by Nick O'Leary (Library Manager).
- One ESP32 DevKit (any 30-pin ESP32-WROOM-32 board), push buttons, and a
  relay module (or LEDs) — one relay channel / LED per circuit below.

## 2. Configure (required before flashing)

Open `controller.ino` and edit the clearly marked `USER CONFIGURATION` block
near the top:

```cpp
static const char* WIFI_SSID       = "YOUR_WIFI_SSID";
static const char* WIFI_PASSWORD   = "YOUR_WIFI_PASSWORD";
static const char* MQTT_BROKER_HOST = "YOUR_MQTT_BROKER_HOST";
static const uint16_t MQTT_BROKER_PORT = 1883;
```

`MQTT_BROKER_HOST` is the machine running the Fanoos Mosquitto broker
(e.g. `docker compose up mosquitto`). Use that machine's **LAN IP** — not
`localhost`, which from the ESP32's point of view is the ESP32 itself.

## 3. Wiring plan

All buttons are wired the same way: one leg to the GPIO pin, the other leg to
**GND** (the firmware uses internal pull-ups; no external resistor needed).
Unused button pins can simply be left unwired.

### Controller button

| ESP32 pin | Direction | Connects to |
|---|---|---|
| GPIO {{.ControllerButtonPin}} | input (pull-up) | Momentary push button → GND |

Pressing it publishes a short `pressed:true` pulse on the controller's state
topic.

### Circuits ({{.CircuitCount}})

{{- range .Circuits}}

#### Circuit {{.Index}} — {{.Name}} (GUID `{{.GUID}}`)

| ESP32 pin | Direction | Connects to |
|---|---|---|
{{- if .IsBuiltinLED }}
| GPIO {{.LightPin}} | output | Nothing — this is the dev kit's **built-in LED**, so this circuit works with no external hardware |
{{- else }}
| GPIO {{.LightPin}} | output | Relay module IN{{.Index}} (or LED + ~330Ω resistor → GND) |
{{- end }}
| GPIO {{.ButtonPin}} | input (pull-up) | Momentary push button → GND (optional local toggle) |
{{- end}}

Polarity per circuit (the `activeHigh` column in `CIRCUITS[]`):
{{- range .Circuits}}
- Circuit {{.Index}} ({{.Name}}): **active-HIGH — HIGH = light on (GPIO {{.LightPin}})**
{{- end}}

> All Fanoos light circuits are active-HIGH: the output goes HIGH to turn
> the light on. Only if you wire a non-standard active-LOW relay module
> should you flip that circuit's `activeHigh` value in the `CIRCUITS[]` table.

### Power

| ESP32 pin | Connects to |
|---|---|
| VIN / 5V | 5V supply (or just use the USB port while testing) |
| GND | Common ground with the relay module and buttons — **must** be shared |

Powering relays from the ESP32's 3V3 pin is not recommended; use the 5V rail
and a supply rated for the relay coil count.

## 4. Customizing pins / circuit order

Everything a technical person needs to change lives in **one table** at the
top of `controller.ino`:

```cpp
static Circuit CIRCUITS[] = {
  { "<light guid>", "<name>", <lightPin>, <activeHigh>, <buttonPin>, false, false, false },
  ...
};
```

- **Change a pin** — edit the `lightPin` / `buttonPin` number in that row
  (and move the wire, per section 3).
- **Reorder circuits** — reorder the rows; nothing else depends on position.
- **Add/remove a circuit** — add/remove a whole row. `CIRCUIT_COUNT` derives
  itself. When adding, use the device GUID of the light from the server UI.
- The controller's own button pin is just above the table:
  `CONTROLLER_BUTTON_PIN`.

Safe-pin notes for a standard ESP32-WROOM-32 DevKit:

- **Input-only pins GPIO 34–39** cannot drive outputs and have no internal
  pull-ups — don't use them for this firmware (buttons rely on INPUT_PULLUP).
- **Avoid GPIO 0 and GPIO 12** (boot strapping); GPIO 2 and 5 are used here
  the same way the reference design uses them.
- Everything else on the board's long edges (13–19, 21–23, 25–27, 32, 33)
  is fair game.

## 5. Flash and test

1. Configure section 2, then upload `controller.ino` to the board
   (Board: "ESP32 Dev Module").
2. Open Serial Monitor at **115200** baud. On boot you should see:

   ```
   [boot] fanoos controller starting
   [boot] env=... controller=... circuits=...
   [wifi] connected, IP=...
   [mqtt] connected
   ```

3. In the Fanoos UI, toggle one of the lights linked to this controller —
   the matching output should change and Serial Monitor should log `[cmd]`.
4. Press a wired push button — the light toggles locally even with the
   network down.

## MQTT reference

| Topic | Direction | Payload |
|---|---|---|
| `home/{{.EnvironmentID}}/device/{{.ControllerGUID}}/state` | controller → server | `{"type":"controller","pressed":bool,"online":bool,"ts":unix}` |
| `home/{{.EnvironmentID}}/device/<light-guid>/cmd` | server → circuit | `{"on":bool}` |
| `home/{{.EnvironmentID}}/device/<light-guid>/state` | circuit → server | `{"type":"light","on":bool,"online":bool,"ts":unix}` |

All messages are retained, QoS 1. Replace `<light-guid>` with each circuit's
GUID from the tables above.
